In this present investigation, aluminium $(\mathsf{A l}^{3+})$ ) fabricated 2-aminobenzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (ABDC) namely Al@ABDC metal organic frameworks (MOFs) was developed for defluoridation studies. The unique advantages of developed MOFs possess high selectivity, high porosity and enhanced surface area but the developed powder form of A $@$ ABDC MOFs has several limitations in field applications like slow filtration and column blockage. To prevail over these troubles, biopolymer namely chitosan (CS) supported Al $@$ ABDC MOFs namely Al@ABDC-CS beads were developed for effective fluoride adsorption from water. The synthesized Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads were employed for the retention of fluoride in batch level. The defluoridation capacities (DCs) of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were found to be 4880 and $4900\ \mathrm{mgF^{-}\ k g^{-1}}$ respectively. The influencing parameters of adsorption method namely agitation time, adsorbent dosage, initial fluoride concentration, pH, co-existing an­ ions and temperature were exploit to get utmost defluoridation capacity (DC) of Al@ABDC-CS beads. The experimental data of Al@ABDC-CS beads have been evaluated utilizing Langmuir, Fruendlich and DubininRadushkevich (D-R) isotherms. The defluoridation nature of Al@ABDC-CS beads was determined by the ther­ modynamic parameters. The order of reaction of Al@ABDC-CS beads was studied using various kinetic models. The regeneration and field water studies of $\mathbf{Al}@$ ABDC-CS beads were also carried out to check their reusability and suitability at field conditions.
## 1. Introduction
Nowadays, water is the most fundamental resources for various living organisms on earth. Fluoride is the essential ion with dual char­ acter in the drinking water. It might be infected by natural sources like fluoride bearing minerals, rock, soil-, etc., and the fluoride enters into human life through water and it exists in various forms in both nonliving and living things. Even though fluoride is a unique ion depends upon the particular fluoride level in drinking water. The WHO suggests that tolerance level of fluoride ions in drinkable water is $1.5\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ [1]. Below the permissible limit $(\leq1.5\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})$ of fluoride in drinking water beneficial for dental and bone mineralization in the human body and above the suggested level $\mathsf{\Gamma}(\geq1.5\mathrm{\mg\L}^{-1})$ causes fluorosis, dental and bone problems [2]. Hence, removal of fluoride in drinking water is very essential one and a very challenging task for researchers. Numerous strategies have been tried for fluoride removal such as reverse osmosis, chemical precipitation, ion exchange, electrocoagulation, membrane filtration and sedimentation [3–7]. These methods have some draw­ backs such as removal efficiency is low, high cost, energy needed and operation is difficult [8]. Defluoridation using adsorption method have received more attention because of removal efficiency is high, very easy operation, chemical usages is less, economical one, eco-friendly, very simple and capable to recycle [9]. Numerous adsorbents have been used such as clay, carbon materials, metal oxides and polymer-based mate­ rials are utilized for fluoride removal [10–13]. Some of the adsorbent materials have disadvantages like low adsorption capacity, poor recy­ clability and poor selectivity [14]. Hence, searching for the good adsorbent with higher adsorption capacity is extremely mandatory one.
In this study, emerging and innovative class of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) were utilized for defluoridation studies. MOFs are defined as metal ions linked with organic ligands to create three dimensional co-ordination networks solids and it is also known as porous co-ordination polymer networks [15]. These MOFs materials possess larger surface area and highly porous nature [16]. These porous MOFs have a lot of applications in different fields such as proton con­ duction, catalysis, adsorption, gas separation, sensors, gas storage and drug delivery [17–19].
Al(III) is a plentiful and economical metal possess very strong bond strength with fluoride [20]. Activated alumina is the mostly utilized as adsorbent material for fluoride removal and Al(III) has magnificent adsorption character for low concentration pollutants [21]. Only limited reports are available for the use of MOFs in water and waste-water treatment. Recently, Yusuff et al. developed copper based MOFs for Cd (II) adsorption [22]. Chowdhury et al. synthesized metal organic framework-graphene oxide nanocomposites for arsenic removal [23]. Liu et al., reported chromium based MOFs for uranium adsorption [24]. Nguyen et al., have studied iron-MOFs for organic contaminant removal [25]. Copper-benzenetricarboxylates based MOF was reported by Mal­ eki et al., to adsorption of hexavalent chromium ions from wastewater [26]. Huang et al. developed core-shell boehmite for defluoridation of water [27]. Pigatto et al. prepared calcinated sludge as low cost and ecofriendly material for fluoride retention from ground water [28]. Chaudhary et al. fabricated nanoparticles and its polymeric nano­ composites (Fe-Al-Mn nanoparticles and Fe-Al-Mn@CS nanocomposite) for defluoridation [29]. Murambasvina et al. synthesized hyacinth beads doped with oxides for fluoride removal [30]. Wang et al., developed 3D porous Ca-tailored Mg-Zr based mixed metal oxide for fluoride removal [31]. Until now, an inadequate number of reports were executed to­ wards toxic/fluoride ion adsorption using MOFs and polymer based materials. Hence, in this investigation Al@ABDC MOFs developed for effective fluoride removal. In addition, powder form of Al@ABDC MOFs may create pressure drop during filtration process. To overcome this problem, biopolymer based hybrid beads was developed for fluoride retention.
Natural polysaccharides chitosan (CS) was obtained from deacety­ lation of chitin [32]. These CS have the active functional groups namely hydroxyl (-OH) and amine $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ to which they could be probably altered for removal of toxic/fluoride ions [33,34]. Chitosan based ma­ terials possess good adsorption capacity in both acidic and basic me­ dium. In addition, CS acquires the brittleness nature, easy gel formation and poor mechanical strength [35]. To overwhelm such blockage, bio­ polymeric hybrid materials were developed. In addition, the CS loaded hybrid materials were developed with different structural forms such as sponge, powder, hybrid beads, etc., [36]. Hence, in this investigation developed Al@ABDC MOFs were cross-linked with CS to form Al@ABDC-CS hybrid beads which possess easily separable nature, enhanced stability with high DC, conquer the pressure drop and column filtration (slow filtration) problems. Moreover, biopolymeric hybrid materials possess some unique advantages like biodegradable, nontoxic, biocompatible, bio-functionality and environmentally friendly [37].
Hence, in this present study investigated on the fluoride adsorptive performance of $\bar{\mathsf{A l}}^{3+}$ fabricated ABDC incorporated CS namely Al@ABDC-CS beads under batch conditions. The physicochemical properties of Al@ABDC-CS beads were studied by XRD, FTIR, TGA, EDAX and SEM studies. Moreover, the promising fluoride removal mechanism of Al@ABDC-CS beads was also explained. The adsorption isotherms, temperature, kinetics, reusability and field study of Al@ABDC-CS beads towards fluoride adsorption was performed.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Aluminium nitrate nonahydrate $(\geq98\%)$ , sodium chloride $({\geq}99\%)$ , 2-aminobenzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (ABDC) $(99\%)$ , sodium hydrox­ ide $(\geq99.0\%)$ , ethanol $(96\%)$ , sodium nitrate $(\geq99\%)$ ), acetic acid $(99.8\%)$ , sodium sulphate $(\geq99.0\%)$ , anhydrous potassium dihydrogen phosphate $(99.9\%)$ , sodium bicarbonate $(\geq99.7\%)$ , and $^{N,N}$ -dime­ thylformamide (N,N-DMF)) $(98.00\%)$ were obtained from Merck, India. Deacetylated chitosan (CS) was acquires from Kerala, India (Pelican Biotech and Chemicals Labs).
## 2.2. Development of the adsorbents
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of Al@ABDC MOFs
$\mathsf{A l@B D C}$ MOFs was formulated with the solvothermal method. Briefly, ABDC $(0.87~\mathrm{g})$ and aluminium nitrate nonahydrate $_{(1.22g)}$ was entirely mixed in N,N-DMF $\mathrm{(60~mL)}$ at ambient temperature and this mixture was magnetically stirred very well. Then, the obtained homo­ geneous dissolved solution was transferred into autoclave (Teflon lined) and heated to $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Then, the resultant product material was centrifuged and followed by washed with double distilled water. Then acquired white powder of Al@ABDC MOFs was dehydrated at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ in oven and utilized for fluoride removal studies.
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of Al@ABDC-CS beads
About $_{2g}$ of chitosan (CS) flakes was completely dissolved in acetic acid $(2\%)$ . Then, $_{1\mathrm{g}}$ of the prepared Al $@$ ABDC MOFs was dispersed with double distilled water and gradually discharged into above CS solution with vigorous stirring for $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ . After that, the attained Al $@$ ABDC-CS homogeneous solution was slowly dropped into NaOH solution to get the usable and separable Al@ABDC-CS beads and they are settled in the same NaOH solution upto $24\mathrm{~h~}$ for intensification function. Finally, the developed Al@ABDC-CS beads were filtered, dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ and used for fluoride adsorption studies. The synthesis of Al@ABDC-CS beads was shown in Fig. 1.
## 2.3. Batch studies for defluoridation
The fluoride removal studies were performed by batch scale to determine the effect of shaking time, Al@ABDC-CS beads dosage, adsorbate $\mathsf{p H}$ , challenging ions, initial fluoride ion solution and tem­ perature. The defluoridation experiments were done with the addition of Al@ABDC-CS beads materials $_{(0.1\ g)}$ into initial fluoride concentration $\scriptstyle\left(50\ \mathrm{mL}\right.$ of $10\ \mathrm{mg\L^{-1}}.$ ) was kept in iodine flask $\ensuremath{\mathrm{(250~mL)}}$ ) at neutral pH condition. Subsequently, the mixture of solutions was shaken in ther­ mostat shaker rotated with constant speed $(200~\mathrm{rpm})$ at room temper­ ature. The adsorbate pH was neutralized by addition of NaOH/HCl (0.1 mol) solution. The adsorption isotherms and kinetic experiments have been performed by varied initial fluoride concentrations (8 to $14~\mathrm{mg}$ ${\bf L}^{-1}$ ) for checking the defluoridation capacity (DC) of developed Al@ABDC-CS beads at various temperature conditions (303, 313, and 323 K). The DC of Al@ABDC-CS beads can be determined by the equa­ tion as follows,
$$
\mathrm{Defluoridation\capacity{\Sigma}(D C)}=\frac{C_{\mathrm{i}}-C_{e}}{m}V\times1000\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{F^{-}k g^{-1}}
$$
where, $\mathrm{C_{i}}$ and ${\sf C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ is initial and final fluoride concentration $(\mathrm{mgL^{-1}})$ , V is the volume of fluoride solution (L) and $\mathbf{m}$ is weight of the Al@ABDC-CS beads (g).
## 2.4. Analysis and statistical tools
The concentration of fluoride and solution $\mathfrak{p H}$ was checked by Thermo Orion Benchtop multiparameter kit (Model: VERSA STAR 92) using fluoride ion-selective electrode (9609 BNWP) and $\mathfrak{p H}$ electrode (Thermo Orion, USA) [38]. The pH drift method was utilized to prove the $\mathsf{p H}$ at zero point charge $\mathrm{(pH_{zpc})}$ of Al@ABDC-CS beads. All other water quality parameters namely dissolved oxygen, total hardness, $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ and total dissolved solids were checked by the standard technique [39].
The calculations were made by Microcal Origin (Version 15.0) soft­ ware. The best fittingness and the finest pertinent model towards defluoridation were scrutinized by the standard numerical tools viz., chi-square $(\chi^{2})$ analysis, regression correlation co-efficient $(\boldsymbol{\mathbf{r}})$ , and standard deviation (sd). These statistical tools were exploited to prove the apt isotherm and kinetic models towards defluoridation experiments.
## 2.5. Adsorbent characterization studies
To determine the functional groups present in the Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were characterized using Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectrometer (Thermo Nicolet 380 model). The different elements present in the synthesized Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were checked with Energy dispersive X-ray analyzer (EDAX) spectrum [Model: Bruker Nano GMBH]. The surface morphology of the prepared Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were explored with field emission-scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) [Model: Quanta FEG 250]. For determination of thermal stability of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads, a thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) [Model: Uni­ versal V4.5A TA] was employed. The crystalline nature of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were determined by X-ray diffraction (XRD) studies (PAnalytic X' Pert Pro Powder XRD).
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Physicochemical characterization of Al@ABDC-CS beads
## 3.1.1. Functional group analysis
FTIR spectra were employed to detect the occurrence of functional groups present in $\mathbf{Al}@$ ABDC MOFs, CS, Al@ABDC-CS beads and fluoride adsorbed Al@ABDC-CS beads which are depicted in Fig. 2a. The sig­ nificant bands present at 1410 and $1589~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associated to the $\cos00^{-}$ (carboxylate) symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibrations of amino-benzenedicarboxylate (ABDC) [40]. The corresponding absorp­ tion band present at $3425~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is related to $-\mathrm{oH}$ vibration (stretching mode). For Al@ABDC MOFs, a series of bands detected at the ranges from 1400 to $1700~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was noticed in $-\mathrm{c}=0$ (carbonyl) groups that is coordinated with ${\mathbb A}1^{3+}$ and the $-\mathrm{c}=0$ of DMF which is sticking to channel of $\mathbf{A}\mathbb{Q}$ ABDC MOFs [41]. For Al@ABDC MOFs, vibrational bands of the carboxyl groups were observed from 1400 to $1600~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . For the jump $\mathtt{C-O}$ group, asymmetrical and symmetrical stretching occurred from 1400 to $1460~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the stretching vibra­ tion of the strong bands occurred at 1582 and $1668~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ respectively, which indicates that aluminium ion coordinated with ABDC species [42]. Also, the characteristic band of Al@ABDC MOFs, the stretching vibration of Al-OH was noticed at 1336 and $1340~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The significant
Al–O exhibits bands at $1000{-}1100\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Particularly, the absorption bands of carbonyl group symmetry stretching vibration was present between 1400 and $1440~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . The dissymmetry stretching vibration of the – $\scriptstyle\mathtt{C}=0$ (carbonyl) group bands was detected at 1500 and $1600\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and the $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ stretching vibration bands was noticed at 3380 and 3500 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ group band was noted at $3230~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and it was correlated to ${\tt N}{\mathrm{-}}{\tt H}$ bonding (asymmetric and symmetrical stretching) of Al@ABDC MOFs [43]. The bands at 1512, $1524\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicate the lesser frequency state, related to the bending vibration of $\tt N-H$ and aromatic amines $\mathsf{C-N}$ stretching vibration. The band observed at $1435\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to $\mathtt{C-O}$ group of carboxylates group present in Al@ABDC MOFs and the bands noted at $1381\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ denotes C–C vibrational mode of Al@ABDC MOFs [44].
For the FTIR spectra of CS, the wide band was noticed at $3441~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was primarily due to $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O-H}$ overlapped with $\tt N-H$ stretching vibration. The characteristic bands noticed at 1652, 1073 and $2875~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ may be classified as the respective N–H, C–O–C and $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{H}$ groups' vibration [45]. For A $@$ ABDC-CS composite beads, the significant bands present at
1410 and $1589~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were associated to the $\mathsf{c o o^{-}}$ (carboxylate) was also reappeared in $\mathbf{Al}@ $ ABDC-CS beads. The corresponding absorption band present at $3424~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ may be related to stretching vibration of –OH was also reappeared. The stretching vibration of the strong bands was occurred at 1582 and $1668~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ respectively, which indicates that aluminium ion coordinated with ABDC species was also reappeared. In addition the corresponding bands of CS groups were appeared in Al@ABDC-CS beads and these results indicates that the efficiently CS groups were incorporated with Al@ABDC MOFs. For fluoride treated Al@ABDC-CS beads, the corresponding absorption band present at $3424~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ might be related to stretching vibration of $-\mathrm{OH}$ intensity was reduced which indicated –OH groups plays important function in fluoride removal. The shrunken of bands indicates that protonated ${-}0\mathrm{{H}_{2}^{+}}$ groups of CS in Al@ABDC-CS beads were attached with fluoride ion through electrostatic attraction mechanism [46]. The stretching vi­ bration of the strong bands occurred at 1582 and $1668~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ respec­ tively which indicates that aluminium ion coordinated with ABDC species were also diminished due to hard acid of ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ binds with fluoride (hard base) through electrostatic attraction and complexation [47]. The $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ stretching vibration bands of Al@ABDC-CS beads were noticed at 3380 and $3500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and these intensities were also shrunken which may due to the $\mathrm{-N{H_{3}}^{+}}$ (protonated) binds with fluoride (negatively charged) by means of electrostatic interaction. Hence, the obtained FTIR result shows that electrostatic interaction and complexation mechanism plays an essential role in the defluoridation process.
## 3.1.2. XRD investigation
The XRD patterns of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads ma­ terials were examined with XRD analysis. The very sharp intensity peaks obtained in the XRD pattern revealed the highly crystalline character of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads materials were demonstrated in Fig. 2b. The XRD patterns of Al@ABDC MOFs attributes high intensity peaks occurred at $2\Theta\:=\:13.16^{\circ}$ , $21.20^{\circ}$ , $26.28^{\circ}$ and $29.32^{\circ}$ and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads characteristics high intensity peaks noted at $2\Theta=$ $12.25^{\circ}$ , $18.15^{\circ}$ , $24.15^{\circ}~27.12^{\circ}$ and $30.08^{\circ}$ which indicates the prepared Al@ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads possess highly crystalline nature [48].
## 3.1.3. TGA analysis
The mass of developed Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads possess great thermal stability nature in high temperature conditions (c. f. Fig. 3). The Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads could nearly decay on two stages at 200 and $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The weight loss of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads before $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was possibly because of volatilization of vapor molecules $\mathrm{(H_{2}O)}$ in the adsorbents. The presence of major and huge weight loss arisen between 150 and $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ could due to $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ groups in Al@ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads [49]. The more weight loss with Al@ABDC-CS beads than Al@ABDC MOFs de­ notes the successful loading of CS. The total weight losses of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads in the ranges between 150 and $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was around $54.5\%$ and $74.2\%$ , respectively.
## 3.1.4. FE-SEM analysis
The morphological nature of the prepared Al@ABDC MOFs, Al@ABDC-CS beads, fluoride adsorbed Al@ABDC MOFs and fluoride adsorbed Al@ABDC-CS beads were described by SEM analysis. Fig. $\mathsf{4a}$ b, c exhibits SEM micro-graphs propose that crystalline Al@ABDC MOFs particles were produced. It could be identified that several elongated strips at 10, 5 and $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ size of Al $@$ ABDC crystals were obviously visible and most of the particles were rod shape which was uniformly distrib­ uted and was not aggregation was depicted in Fig. 4a, b, c. Fig. 4d shows SEM micrographs images of fluoride adsorbed Al $@$ ABDC MOFs at $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ scale which shows that surface morphology of Al $@$ ABDC MOFs was tailored may due to fluoride filled the pores of Al@ABDC MOFs.
Fig. 4e and f reveals the digital image of Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads and dried Al@ABDC-CS beads respectively. Fig. $^{4g}$ and h exhibits the SEM image of Al@ABDC-CS beads possess highly porous and crystalline na­ ture at $5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . After fluoride adsorption porous crystalline nature was diminished due to fluoride ions occupy more active sites in Al@ABDCCS beads which is represented in Fig. 4h. Hence, surface morphology changes indicate that fluoride adsorption may occur in the prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads.
## 3.1.5. EDAX analysis
The presence of elements in Al@ABDC MOFs, Al@ABDC-CS beads and fluoride adsorbed Al@ABDC-CS beads were examined by EDAX spectra which results are appeared in Fig. 5. The vital elements in O, C, Al and N with atomic percentage of $33.20\pm0.11$ , $29.04\pm0.03$ and $24.09\pm0.02$ , $8.41~\pm~0.03\%$ respectively was present in Al@ABDC MOFs (c.f. Fig. 5a). For Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads, the important elements namely O, C, Al and N with the atomic percentage of $37.20\pm0.08$ , $22.04\pm0.02$ and $21.09\pm0.04$ , $16.41\pm0.02\%$ respectively was present in Al@ABDC-CS beads (c.f. Fig. 5b). The nitrogen intensity and atomic percentage of Al@ABDC-CS beads enhanced than $\mathop{\mathrm{Al}}(\overline{{\alpha}})$ ABDC MOFs which may due to CS encapsulate with Al@ABDC MOFs, the $\mathbf{Al}@$ ABDCCS beads possess amine groups because of nitrogen atomic percentage enhanced than Al@ABDC-CS beads. Hence, this result indicates CS efficiently encapsulated with Al $@$ ABDC MOFs. In addition, the oxygen group intensity increases which may due to excess $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups present in CS of Al@ABDC-CS beads. For fluoride treated Al@ABDC-CS beads found to have Al, C, O, N with fluoride peaks confirmed that the fluoride adsorption was take place onto developed Al@ABDC-CS beads. For fluoride treated Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads, the corresponding elements such as O, C, Al, N and F with atomic percentage of $35.20\pm0.08$ , $21.04\pm$ 0.02, $16.09\pm0.04$ , $13.41\pm0.02$ and $11.05\%$ respectively presented in Fig. 5c . In addition, the significant new fluoride peak was present with $11.05\%$ of atomic weight which indicates fluoride adsorption was occurred onto Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads.
## 3.2. Effect of shaking time
The synthesized Al@ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads were investigated to determine the shaking time which diverse from 10 to 60 min on DCs as depicted in Fig. 6(a). The shaking time of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads from 10 to 30 and 10 to $20~\mathrm{min}$ of DCs was increased due to more active sites in Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads respectively. For Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads, after 30 to 60 and 20 to $60\ \mathrm{min}$ of DCs no significant changes or slightly increment of adsorption was observed due to adsorption binding sites more than the initial fluoride solution. The adsorption equilibrium or saturation attained at $30\ \mathrm{min}$ for Al@ABDC MOFs and $20\ \mathrm{min}$ for Al@ABDC-CS beads respectively. The prepared Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads possess improved DCs of 4880 and $4900\mathrm{mgF^{-}k g^{-1}}$ within 30 and $20~\mathrm{min}$ respectively. The ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ was formed as a surface complex with –COOH groups of ABDC and shaped as ${\mathrm{AlF}}_{3}$ in the aqueous solution via electrostatic interaction which may lead to attain the utmost fluoride adsorption capacity. In addition, the prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads possess the maximum DC than Al@ABDC MOFs which may because of the extra $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ and –OH groups were present in the Al@ABDC-CS beads and these groups $\mathrm{-NH_{2}}$ and –OH of Al@ABDCCS beads were protonated and protonated $\mathrm{-NH_{3}}^{+}$ and ${\mathrm{-oH_{2}}}^{+}$ was efficiently adsorbed fluoride through electrostatic interaction [50]. In addition, metal cluster such as aluminium present in both Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads which was adsorbed fluoride ion through electrostatic interaction by HSAB principle [51]. The DC of Al@ABDCCS beads higher than Al $@$ ABDC MOFs due the excess amount of amine groups present in $\mathbf{Al}@$ ABDC-CS beads. Hence, the optimum shaking time of $30\mathrm{min}$ for Al@ABDC MOFs and $20\mathrm{min}$ for $\mathbf{Al}@.$ ABDCCS beads was picked for further defluoridation studies of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads.
## 3.3. Effect of MOFs and beads dosage
Al@ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads dosage was studied using various amounts of adsorbent dosage viz., 0.05, 0.10, 0.15, 0.20 and $0.25g$ respectively. Initially, the fluoride adsorption capacity was found to increase rapidly from 0.05 to $0.10\mathrm{~g~}$ because Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads have more attractive active sites in the materials due to greater DC occurred at initial stage [52] which was shown in Fig. 6(b). After $_{\mathbf{\delta}_{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}}$ of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads dosage, no rise in DC was observed due to availability of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads dosage active sites greater than adsorbate solution. Therefore, $_{0.1\mathrm{~g~}}$ dosage of Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads was optimized for upcoming adsorption experiments.
## 3.4. Influence of solution pH
The fluoride removal by Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads achieved by different fluoride solution pH levels like 3 to 11 respectively and other factors are reserved as constant at ambient temperature (c.f. Fig. 6(c)). The fluoride solution pH was attuned or neutralized by accumulation of $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ of HCl/NaOH. Fig. 6(c) exposed that the DC of Al@ABDC MOFs and $A l@_{\mathbf{ABDC-CS}}$ beads are affected by pH medium. The pH notably manipulate the solid/liquid boundary which terminates to fluoride adsorption is significantly pH dependent. The developed Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads have better fluoride removal that happened at pH 7. The DC was rather influenced with acidic as well as alkaline medium. The differences in the DCs of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were not significant. The adsorbate solution final solution pH value initiate to be in the level of 6–8 irrespective of the initial pH range which denotes the Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads suitable at acidic as well as alkaline situation [53]. In addition at acidic pH, A $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads happen to pro­ tonated surface thus, fluoride may adsorb onto Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads surface by electrostatic adsorption. After the pH enhances above 7, fluoride adsorption slowly reduce owing to the ex­ istence of a leading competitive $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions at basic pH medium [54].
The significance $\mathrm{\pH_{zpc}}$ values of Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were found to be 6.15 and 6.35 respectively, which are repre­ sented in Fig. 6(d). It indicates that if $\mathrm{\pH<\mathsf{pH}_{\mathrm{zpc}};}$ , the Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads have positively charged adsorbent surface adsorbed fluoride through electrostatic attraction. At the same time, DC was reduced after $\mathtt{p H7}$ owing to a challenge between the fluoride and hydroxyl ions during $\mathrm{pH}>\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{zpc}}$ . Moreover, the final adsorbate $\mathsf{p H}$ of fluoride solution was found to be $\mathrm{pH}7$ towards defluoridation, which exhibits Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads were utilized at different pH medium.
## 3.5. Influence of interfering anions
Normally the drinkable water not only contains fluoride anion and also the occurrence of other co-existing anions namely sulphate, chlo­ ride, carbonate, bicarbonate, phosphate, and nitrate investigated which results are appeared in Fig. 6(e). The initial adsorbate concentration (10 $\mathbf{mgL}^{-1}]$ ) and co-anion concentration level fixed as $200\mathrm{{mgL}^{-1}}$ range and other factors kept as constant at $303~\mathrm{K}.$ The chloride, sulphate, phos­ phate, carbonate and nitrate anions present in the water slightly changes of DC was occurred and the presence of bicarbonate ions DC was reduced because of the bicarbonate ions affect the fluoride adsorption process onto Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads due to hydroxyl groups weakening the active sites which is shown in Fig. 6(e). Besides, the selectivity nature of $\mathbf{Al}@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads to­ wards defluoridation was carried out using different adsorbate solution such as bicarbonate, sulphate, carbonate, nitrate, phosphate, chromate and chloride. Among them, synthesized Al@ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDCCS beads possess fluoride interaction was higher than other adsorbate concentration which specifies selectivity towards fluoride adsorption onto Al $@$ ABDC MOFs and Al@ABDC-CS beads which shown in Fig. 6(f). Since Al@ABDC-CS beads possesses higher DC than Al@ABDC MOFs, further studies were limited to Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads only.
## 3.6. Adsorption isotherms studies
To measure the fluoride uptake of the prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads was utilized three varied isotherms specifically D-R [55], Langmuir [56] and Fruendlich [57] adsorption isotherm models have been assumed. The Fruendlich adsorption isotherms constants of Al@ABDC-CS beads were shown in Table 1. The linear plot as $\log{\mathfrak{q}}_{\mathrm{e}}$ vs $\log\mathrm{C_{e}}$ which desig­ nates the suitability of Fruendlich model. The n and $1/\mathfrak{n}$ values are attaining from 1 to 10 and 0–1 verify the favorable condition for the fluoride adsorption process with expands in temperature and values of $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{F}}$ increases signify that the fluoride adsorption on Al@ABDC-CS com­ posite beads exhibit its endothermic nature [58]. The fittingness of Langmuir isotherm indicates the linear plot of $\mathrm{C_{e}/q_{e}}$ vs ${\sf C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ . The Langmuir constants like b and $\boldsymbol{{\mathrm Q}}^{\mathrm{o}}$ values were depicted in Table 1. The $\mathtt{R_{L}}$ values ranges lying from 0 to 1 signify favorable condition of fluoride adsorp­ tion at various temperature conditions (303, 313 and 323 K).
The $\log{\mathfrak{q}}_{\mathrm{e}}$ vs $\varepsilon^{2}$ is linear plot, which points out the fitness of D-R isotherm model. The experimental value of E, $X_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{DR}}$ is described in Table 1. In addition, E values ranges less than $8.0\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ signifies physisorption and above $9~\mathrm{\kJ~\mol^{-1}}$ specifies chemisorption. The attained values of E ranges over $9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ indicates fluoride adsorption on Al@ABDC-CS beads follows chemisorption mechanism. Hence, from Table 1 results, the low sd and $\chi^{2}$ ; the high r values assigned that Langmuir isotherm as the most suitable model towards fluoride adsorption on Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads.
## 3.7. Thermodynamic parameters
To measure, the thermodynamic factors were correlated with the fluoride adsorption such as $\Delta\mathsf{G}^{\circ}$ , $\Delta S^{\circ}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ by Khan and Singh system [59]. The obtained data of thermodynamic studies results were exhibi­ ted in Table 2. The $\Delta\mathsf{G}^{\circ}$ (negative values) for Al $@$ ABDC-CS beads ver­ ifies the spontaneity of defluoridation process. The $\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}$ (positive values) for Al@ABDC-CS beads proves the fluoride removal method was endo­ thermic nature [60]. The $\Delta S^{\circ}$ (positive values) for Al@ABDC-CS beads which is assessing of randomness occurred at adsorbent-adsorbate in­ terfaces in fluoride adsorption point out the fluoride adsorption route is spontaneity process and stable.
## 3.8. Adsorption kinetic studies of Al@ABDC MOFs
The mainly used kinetic models (pseudo-first-order, pseudo-secondorder, particle diffusion and intraparticle diffusion models) were explored to aptness the experimental values of the synthesized Al@ABDC-CS beads. The reaction-based models like pseudo-first-order [61] and pseudo-second-order model [62] were exploited to explicate the adsorbent–adsorbate interface adsorption process. The aptness of linear plot obtained using formula like log $\left(\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}^{-}}\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}\right)$ vs t (pseudo-firstorder) and $\mathrm{{\Deltat/q_{t}}}$ vs t (pseudo-second-order) for defluoridation at different temperatures which provides experimental values of the pseudo-firstorder values are r and $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{ad}};$ pseudo-second-order values are h, k, r and $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ of the Al@ABDC-CS beads were portrayed in Table S1. The $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ increased with rise in temperature which demonstrates fluoride adsorption increases with an increase in temperature of Al@ABDC-CS beads.
Defluoridation in solid-adsorbate, the solute transfer was examined by particle diffusion [63] and intraparticle diffusion [64]. The linear plot obtained by log $(1-C_{\mathrm{f}}/\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{e}}})$ vs t (particle diffusion) and $\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ vs $\mathrm{t}^{0.5}$ (intraparticle diffusion) for fluoride adsorption. The constant values of $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{p}},\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{i}}$ and r at 303, 313 and $323\mathrm{K}$ towards both models of Al@ABDC-CS beads for fluoride adsorption was represented in Table S1. From the observed results, lesser sd and high r values demonstrates pseudosecond-order models and intraparticle diffusion were finest fit models towards fluoride adsorption onto Al@ABDC-CS beads.
## 3.9. Defluoridation mechanism of Al@ABDC MOFs
Adsorption mechanism of Al@ABDC-CS beads on fluoride was identified to be electrostatic interaction and complexation mechanism which is depicted in Fig. 7. The –COOH groups of ABDC can be shaped as ${\cdot}{\mathsf{C O O}}^{-}{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ which may easily complex with ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ and fashioned as $\cdot{\bf C}{\bf O}{\bf O}^{-}.....{\bf A}]^{3+}$ . The occurrence of ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ in $\mathbf{Al}@$ ABDC-CS beads makes the higher stability for the prepared MOFs in which ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ plays the strong acidic nature (hard acid or Lewis acid) which often attracts with fluoride (hard base or Lewis base) by the means of electrostatic interaction [65]. The prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads surface have positively charged metal sites $(\mathsf{A l}^{3+})$ easily attract with negatively charged fluoride ion $(\mathrm{F}^{-})$ in adsorbate solution by electrostatic attraction and complexation mech­ anism. The prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads have amine $\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)$ which was protonated $\left(-\mathrm{NH_{3}}^{+}\right).$ and these $\mathrm{NH_{3}}^{+}$ which often attracts with the $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ion via electrostatic adsorption [66] as depicted in Fig. 7. In addition, defluoridation mechanism of Al@ABDC-CS beads was elucidated in FTIR and EDAX investigation (see Sections 3.1.1 and 3.1.4).
## 3.10. Comparison investigation
A comparison of adsorption capacity of many adsorbents towards fluoride removal was presented in Table 3. From Table 3, the synthe­ sized Al@ABDC-CS beads possess higher DC compared to the numerous other adsorbent materials.
## 3.11. Field trail
The field water test sample was collected from fluoride rife area within Dindigul district, Tamilnadu, India. Briefly, the prepared
Al@ABDC-CS beads $_{(0.1\mathrm{~g)~}}$ and field sample $\mathrm{(50~mL)}$ ) shaken with optimized shaking time and after fluoride concentration was analyzed. The fluoride concentration of field water was noticed as $3.37\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ and following Al@ABDC-CS beads added to field water, the fluoride con­ centration was diminished less than the prescribed level (i.e., ${>}1.5~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{L}^{-1}.$ ) of fluoride in potable water by WHO are depicted in Table 4. To this credit, the prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads also reduced other water quality parameters (WQPs) $(\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-}$ , dissolved oxygen, pH, total hardness and total dissolved solids) show the suitability at field studies (c.f. Table 4).
## 3.12. Regeneration studies
The reusability investigation was carried out using fluoride adsorbed Al@ABDC-CS beads with $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ of NaOH [76] at $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ and extreme desorption efficiency of $\operatorname{Al}({\widehat{\underline{{a}}}})$ ABDC-CS beads are shown in Fig. 8. Desorption efficiency of Al@ABDC-CS beads shows reusable upto six cycles. Every cycle the removal efficiency was reduced slightly, after six cycle's removal efficiency completely very low. Hence, the prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads were regenerable upto 6 cycles for adsorptiondesorption studies.
## 4. Conclusions
The synthesized Al@ABDC-CS beads have enhanced DC of 4900 $\mathrm{mgF}^{-1}~\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ within the contact time of $20~\mathrm{min}$ . The physicochemical characterization of the prepared Al@ABDC-CS beads was executed using XRD, TGA, FTIR, EDAX and SEM studies. During solution pH studies, Al@ABDC-CS beads possess a maximum DC at neutral pH conditions. The occurrences of bicarbonate ions slightly diminished DC due to the similar ionic size and the protonation reaction takes place between Al@ABDC-CS beads and fluoride. The Langmuir isotherm was elected as the best fit model for fluoride adsorption onto Al@ABDC-CS beads. The adsorption kinetic studies expose that pseudo-second-order and intra­ particle diffusion models were the finest apt models for fluoride adsorption onto Al@ABDC-CS beads. The result of thermodynamic pa­ rameters studies illustrates the endothermic nature of fluoride adsorp­ tion with spontaneous nature. The feasible adsorption mechanism of Al@ABDC-CS beads was predominantly leads with electrostatic inter­ action and complexation. The recyclability investigation showed that Al@ABDC-CS beads can be reused upto six cycles. The field study of Al@ABDC-CS beads reduced fluoride concentration below the tolerance level. Therefore, Al@ABDC-CS beads were effectively utilized for se­ lective fluoride adsorption.
